1.	Authentication -List the five authentication factors
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
Authentication is the set of methods used to verify that a claimed identity is true. The five factors:
•	Something you know  (password)
•	Something you are  (iris scan)
•	Something you have  (swipe card)
•	Something you do  (gait recognition)
•	The place you are  (a specific terminal)
2.	Explain-features of Symmetric encryption
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
Symmetric encryption uses a single key to both encrypt and decrypt data. Its features:
•	Both parties must share a common key before exchanging information.
•	Keys should be changed regularly to prevent attacks.
•	A robust key-exchange mechanism is needed; because keys change often, this becomes expensive and cumbersome.
3.	Antivirus and  software - Explain benefits of antivirus software
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
Antivirus software is a security program that prevents, detects and removes viruses and other malware. Benefits:
•	Virus and malware protection
•	Protection against spam and pop-ups
•	Web protection
•	Real-time protection
•	Dark web scanning
•	Protection from external devices
4.	Explain behaviors-based detection in antivirus software
Behavioral detection observes how a program actually behaves, rather than just emulating its execution. It looks for suspicious actions such as unpacking malcode, modifying the hosts file or logging keystrokes.
It does not look for known viruses but monitors all programs; for example, if a program tries to write data to an executable, the antivirus flags it and alerts the user. This gives protection against brand-new viruses that are not yet in any virus dictionary.
5.	computer worms?
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
A computer worm is malware whose primary function is to self-replicate and infect other computers while remaining active on infected systems. It duplicates itself to spread to uninfected computers, often by exploiting automatic and invisible parts of the operating system.
6.	What is Spyware software?
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
Spyware is malicious software that secretly enters a computing environment and gathers confidential data without being detected, then passes it on to third parties such as fraudulent advertisers or data farms. It is installed without the user’s permission, and its main purpose is to steal credit card information, banking details and passwords.
7.	What is the Trojans horse?
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
Trojans (Trojan horses) are malicious code, but not viruses: they neither infect other files nor self-replicate like worms. A Trojan works only if the user installs it, e.g. an executable (.exe), an Android package (.apk) or an iOS package (.ipa).
8.	Explain social engineering attack
Mövzu: Kiber hücumlar
Social engineering uses social skills to convince people to reveal credentials or other valuable information. Victims are manipulated into sharing information, downloading software, visiting websites or sending money, compromising personal or organizational security.
Typical attack lifecycle:
1.	Research  – gathering information from social media, company websites, LinkedIn and public records.
2.	Hook  – making contact and building a pretext or rapport.
3.	Play  – exploiting the trust to obtain information or access.
4.	Exit  – ending the interaction without arousing suspicion, often covering tracks.
9.	Explain brute force, dictionary and man in the middle-attacks
•	Brute force  – using computing resources to try every possible password combination; also called a password attack.
•	Dictionary  – a variation of brute force that targets specific accounts and uses a list of commonly used passwords instead of random combinations.
•	Man-in-the-Middle (MiTM)  – the attacker positions themselves between two communicating parties, intercepting and possibly altering their communication, stealing data such as login credentials or financial information.
10.	Explain - backdoor attack
Mövzu: Kiber hücumlar
A backdoor attack occurs when attackers create or use a backdoor to gain remote access to a system by exploiting weaknesses in software, hardware or network infrastructure. It gives continued access without further authentication, letting them control system resources, perform network reconnaissance and install malware.
The consequences can be immediate and severe (a data breach), or appear later when the attacker returns to carry out direct attacks.
11.	Explain DDoS attack and  DoS attack
Mövzu: Kiber hücumlar
•	DoS  – makes a resource (site, application, server) unavailable to legitimate users, e.g. by flooding it with requests or exploiting vulnerabilities.
•	DDoS  – floods the target with malicious traffic from multiple compromised devices (a botnet), causing slow performance or a complete outage.
Main difference: DoS is a system-on-system attack, while DDoS involves many systems attacking a single system.
12.	What is phishing attack?
Mövzu: Kiber hücumlar
Phishing is a cyberattack that uses fraudulent emails, text messages, calls or websites to trick people into sharing sensitive data, downloading malware or otherwise exposing themselves to cybercrime. It is a form of social engineering that relies on human error, fake stories and pressure tactics.
Typically the attacker pretends to be someone trusted (a colleague, boss or well-known brand) and urges the victim to pay an invoice, open an attachment or click a link.
13.	What is the Difference between Information security and cybersecurity?
Mövzu: Kibertəhlükəsizlik
•	Information security  protects information in any format – paper documents, digital data, intellectual property in people’s minds, and verbal or visual communication.
•	Cybersecurity  protects digital assets – from networks and hardware to information processed, stored or transported by internetworked information systems.
14.	What is Parkerian hexad?
Mövzu: Kompüter sistemlərinin təhlükəsizliyinə giriş
•	Possession or control  – the physical disposition of the media on which data is stored; lets us discuss the loss of data in its physical medium.
•	Authenticity  – proper attribution of the owner or creator of data; e.g. an e-mail altered to appear from a different address violates authenticity.
•	Utility  – how useful the data is to us; it varies with the data and its format.
15.	What is the CIA triad?
Mövzu: Kompüter sistemlərinin təhlükəsizliyinə giriş
•	Confidentiality  – protecting data from those not authorized to view it; a necessary component of privacy.
•	Integrity  – preventing data from being changed in an unauthorized or undesirable manner.
•	Availability  – the ability to access data when needed; it can be affected by power loss, OS or application problems, network attacks or system compromise.
16.	The types of security controls
Mövzu: Kompüter sistemlərinin təhlükəsizliyinə giriş
•	Physical controls  – tangible measures that prevent or detect unauthorized access: fences, gates, guards, badges, biometrics, CCTV, motion sensors.
•	Technical controls  – hardware or software mechanisms: authentication solutions, firewalls, antivirus, IDS/IPS, ACLs, encryption.
•	Administrative controls  – policies, procedures and guidelines: hiring and termination, equipment and Internet usage, separation of duties, auditing, security awareness training.
17.	What are Vulnerabilities?
Mövzu: Kompüter sistemlərinin təhlükəsizliyinə giriş
A vulnerability is a weakness or fault in a system or protection mechanism that opens it to attack or damage. It is not the attack or the damage itself, but the gap that makes the attack possible. Classes:
•	Hardware  – susceptibility to humidity/dust, overheating
•	Software  – insufficient testing, insecure coding, design flaws
•	Network  – unprotected communication lines, insecure architecture
•	Personnel  – inadequate recruiting, low security awareness
•	Physical site  – area subject to natural disasters
•	Organizational  – lack of regular audits and continuity plans
18.	What is Information Security information security’s role for organization
Mövzu: Kompüter sistemlərinin təhlükəsizliyinə giriş
Information security means protecting information and information systems from unauthorized access, use, disclosure, disruption, modification or destruction. Its functions for an organization:
•	Protects the organization’s ability to function
•	Protects the data the organization collects and uses
•	Safeguards the technology assets in use
19.	What is Inferential (Blind) Structure Query language (SQl) injection?
Mövzu: Verilənlər bazasının təhlükəsizliyi
The attacker sends payloads to the server and observes its response and behavior to learn about its structure. It is called blind because no data is transferred from the database to the attacker, so results cannot be seen in-band.
20.	What is Cross-Site Scripting  is and describe its types?
Mövzu: Proqram Təhlükəsizliyi
Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a web application vulnerability that allows a third party to execute a script in the user’s browser on behalf of the web application.
Types: Stored XSS (persistent), Reflected XSS (non-persistent), DOM-based XSS.
21.	Explain Role-based access control (RBAC)
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
RBAC assigns access permissions according to a staff member’s job responsibilities. For example, an HR employee needs access to different systems and records than a sales employee; permissions are aligned with each job function.
Orta səviyyə
1.	Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) In a software development company, employees are organized by job position. All developers can read and write source code, all testers can read code and submit bug reports, and all project managers can view reports and approve releases. When a tester is promoted to developer, their permissions change automatically to match the new position — the administrator does not set permissions for each person individually. Which access control model does this describe?
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
Role-Based Access Control (RBAC). Access is based on the job position (developer, tester, project manager), and permissions belong to the role, not the individual, so they change automatically when a person’s role changes.
2.	In a small company, employees are assigned to groups such as “HR”, “Finance”, and “Sales”. Each group is given a fixed set of permissions, and an employee automatically receives all the permissions of the group they belong to. Which access control model does this describe?
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
Role-Based Access Control (RBAC). Employees are assigned to groups (HR, Finance, Sales) and automatically receive all permissions of their group.
3.	In a hospital system, access to patient records is based on job function. Doctors can view and edit medical histories, nurses can view them and add notes, and receptionists can only see appointment times. When a nurse is promoted to a different position, her permissions change automatically to match the new position. Which access control model does this describe?
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
Role-Based Access Control (RBAC). Access is based on job function, and when a nurse is promoted her permissions change automatically with the new position.
