17.	Write an algorithm for the Columnar Transposition - Cipher
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
Input: plaintext P and key k (number of columns). Output: ciphertext C.
1.	Remove spaces from P.
2.	Create a table with k columns and write the letters of P into it row by row.
3.	If the last row is incomplete, leave it empty or pad it with a filler letter.
4.	Read the table column by column (first to last column, top to bottom).
5.	The letters read in this order form the ciphertext C.
18.	What is Out-of-band Structure Query language (SQL) injection?
Mövzu: Verilənlər bazasının təhlükəsizliyi
Out-of-band SQLi is used when the attacker cannot use the same channel to attack and gather results, or when the server is too slow or unstable. It requires certain features to be enabled on the database server.
It relies on the server’s ability to make DNS or HTTP requests: the database “calls home” to an attacker-controlled server, and the attacker reads the stolen data from those requests.
Prevention: never build SQL with untrusted input; restrict database permissions and outbound network access.
19.	What is In-band Structure Query language (SQL) injection?
Mövzu: Verilənlər bazasının təhlükəsizliyi
The attacker uses the same communication channel to launch the attack and gather results. Its simplicity and efficiency make it one of the most common SQLi types. Sub-variations:
•	Error-based SQLi  – causes the database to produce error messages that reveal its structure.
•	Union-based SQLi  – uses the UNION operator to combine several SELECT statements into one HTTP response containing useful data.
20.	What is Structure Query language (SQl) injection and write its types?
Mövzu: Verilənlər bazasının təhlükəsizliyi
SQL injection is a code injection technique used to modify or retrieve data from SQL databases. By inserting SQL statements into an entry field, an attacker can retrieve or destroy data or manipulate it in other ways.
Types: In-band SQLi (classic), Inferential SQLi (blind) and Out-of-band SQLi.
21.	what is Stored XSS ?
Mövzu: Proqram Təhlükəsizliyi
Stored (persistent) XSS is the most damaging type of XSS. The attacker injects a malicious payload, usually JavaScript, which – without input validation – is permanently stored by the application (e.g. in a database via a blog comment or forum post). When a victim opens the page, the payload is served as part of the HTML and runs in their browser.
22.	What is Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is and describe its types?
Mövzu: Proqram Təhlükəsizliyi
Cross-Site Scripting is a web application vulnerability that allows a third party to execute a script in the user’s browser on behalf of the web application. Consequences include account compromise or deletion, privilege escalation and malware infection.
Types: Stored XSS (persistent), Reflected XSS (non-persistent), DOM-based XSS.
23.	What is race condition software vulnerability race conditions?
Mövzu: Proqram Təhlükəsizliyi
Race conditions occur when multiple processes or threads share access to a resource and correct handling depends on the proper order or timing of operations.
Example: an account holds $100. User 1 withdraws $20 and User 2 withdraws $30 at the same time. Both read a $100 balance, so the last update leaves $70 recorded instead of $50.
Race conditions are hard to detect and reproduce; careful resource access that does not depend on timing avoids them.
24.	What is buffer overflow software vulnerability?
Mövzu: Proqram Təhlükəsizliyi
Buffer overflows occur when the size of input data is not properly accounted for. Without bounds checking, e.g. 1000 characters entered into storage allocated for 50, the excess overwrites memory used by other applications or the operating system. An attacker can use this to tamper with other applications or make the OS run their own commands. Proper bounds checking prevents it.
25.	What is the issues related to software security?
Mövzu: Proqram Təhlükəsizliyi
•	Complexity of software  – security holes are harder to find and fix.
•	Evolving threat landscape  – new attacks and vulnerabilities appear constantly.
•	Lack of awareness  – developers and organizations unknowingly run insecure programs.
•	Resource constraints  – small organizations lack security staff and tools.
•	Legacy systems  – outdated software is hard to secure and cannot use modern solutions.
•	Security not a priority  – a survey says 86% of developers do not see security as a top priority.
26.	What is software security? Importance of software security
Mövzu: Proqram Təhlükəsizliyi
Software security is the set of processes and mechanisms used to protect software from unauthorized access, use or destruction, ensuring data is protected in transit and at rest. Its importance:
•	Data protection  – prevents data breaches, identity theft and financial loss.
•	Business continuity  – incidents can halt operations and damage reputation.
•	Regulatory compliance  – non-compliance with data protection laws brings legal liability.
•	User trust  – customers lose faith in a company that mishandles data.
•	Intellectual property protection  – software is often crucial IP that must be guarded from unauthorized use.
27.	What is the Trusted Computing Base?
Mövzu: Əməliyyat sistemlərinin təhlükəsizliyi
The Trusted Computing Base (TCB) is the set of all hardware, firmware and software components critical to a system’s security. It is “trusted” because the security of the whole system depends on it: a bug or compromise in the TCB can make the entire system fail.
In a typical OS it includes the kernel, kernel-mode device drivers, authentication and login mechanisms, access-control enforcement components, and hardware protection features (privilege modes, memory protection).
The design goal is to keep the TCB as small as possible: fewer places for vulnerabilities and easier testing and verification.
28.	Why the operating system is the foundation of security?
Mövzu: Əməliyyat sistemlərinin təhlükəsizliyi
The OS manages the computer’s hardware, and every security layer relies on the layers beneath it; applications, security tools and data all depend on the OS to enforce protection. If the OS is compromised, the protections above it can be bypassed:
•	encryption keys can be read from memory;
•	antivirus can be disabled or hidden from by a rootkit with kernel-level privileges;
•	keystrokes, including passwords, can be intercepted;
•	firewall rules and network settings can be altered.
29.	What is Rule-based access control?
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
Rule-based access control grants access to areas, devices or databases according to a predetermined set of rules, regardless of the person’s role or position. For example, an administrator may allow entry only during business hours (9 a.m. – 5 p.m.). Such rules are not necessarily the same as role-based privileges.
30.	What is Mandatory access control (MAC)?
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
MAC suits organizations with a tiered security clearance system and is popular with government and industries needing strict control of sensitive data (finance, engineering, healthcare). It gives the highest level of data protection: data is classified and security is administered centrally.
Drawbacks: it is hard to implement and manage, can impede data sharing, needs constant rule updates and is rigid.
31.	What is  Attribute-based access control (ABAC)?
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
ABAC is a policy-based approach that grants access based on user characteristics (department, objectives, security clearance) and also on context: secure device and location, and timing in line with policy.
Examples: a professor sees a student’s grades only during the term they teach that student; a doctor can access a patient’s file only when treating that patient and only from a secure location.
32.	What is Discretionary access control (DAC)?
Mövzu: Girişə Nəzarət Modelləri
In DAC the resource’s owner holds the administrative power and can grant or restrict access without central approval. It relies on access control lists (ACLs) or user groups. It is often used for shared documents (e.g. a shared Google Doc).
DAC is a quick way to grant access, but not the most secure, as it gives external actors a faster route to the asset.
33.	Compare Traditional IT network security and Zero trust security model
Mövzu: Təhlükəsizlik Modelləri və freymvorklər
•	Traditional IT network security  – “castle-and-moat”: hard to get in from outside, but everyone inside the network is trusted by default; once an attacker gets in, they have free rein.
•	Zero Trust  – requires strict identity verification for every person and device accessing resources, whether inside or outside the network perimeter; it trusts no one and nothing.
34.	What is Clark-Wilson security model?
Mövzu: Təhlükəsizlik Modelləri və freymvorklər
The Clark-Wilson model is designed for commercial environments. Unlike BLP and Biba, it uses roles, rules and workflows instead of strict security levels to keep data accurate and unaltered. Only trusted actions may change the “safe” data.
•	Subject  – any user requesting data items.
•	Constrained data items (CDIs)  – cannot be accessed directly by the subject; only through the model’s trusted procedures.
•	Unconstrained data items (UDIs)  – can be accessed directly by the subject.
35.	What is Biba security model?
Mövzu: Təhlükəsizlik Modelləri və freymvorklər
The Biba model maintains integrity and works as the reverse of Bell-LaPadula. Rules:
•	Simple Integrity Rule  – read the same or a higher level, not lower (no read down).
•	Star Integrity Rule  – write the same or a lower level, not higher (no write up).
•	Strong Star Integrity Rule  – read and write only at your own level.
36.	What is Bell-La Padula security model?
Mövzu: Təhlükəsizlik Modelləri və freymvorklər
The Bell-La Padula model maintains confidentiality. Rules:
•	Simple Confidentiality Rule  – read the same or a lower level, not higher (no read up).
•	Star Confidentiality Rule  – write the same or a higher level, not lower (no write down).
•	Strong Star Confidentiality Rule  – read and write only at the same level (no read/write up or down).
37.	What is Brewer–Nash (Chinese Wall) model?
Mövzu: Təhlükəsizlik Modelləri və freymvorklər
The Brewer–Nash model protects against conflicts of interest, typically in consulting, auditing or investment firms where one employee must not advise two competing clients.
Example: after reading Bank A’s confidential strategy you may not read Bank B’s, but reading Oil X’s files is fine since oil companies do not compete with banks. At first you are free to choose; once you choose, a “wall” rises around that client’s competitors. You may read a document if (1) you have already read something from the same company, or (2) you have read nothing from any competitor in that conflict class.
38.	What is the state machine model?
Mövzu: Təhlükəsizlik Modelləri və freymvorklər
The system is modelled as moving between “states” (who is reading or editing which file) whenever a user makes a request; some states are safe, others unsafe. Since checking every state is impossible, the “domino” logic is used: if (1) the system starts in a safe state, and (2) every rule moves a safe state to another safe state, the system can never reach an unsafe state.
39.	What are benefits and disadvantages of asymmetric encryption?
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
•	Benefits  – no key distribution problem (no key exchange needed); higher security because private keys are never transmitted; digital signatures let a recipient verify the sender.
•	Disadvantages  – slow compared to symmetric encryption, so unsuitable for bulk data; a lost private key means messages cannot be decrypted; public keys are not authenticated, so users must verify that a public key belongs to the claimed person.
40.	How does asymmetric-cryptography work?
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
Asymmetric encryption uses a mathematically related pair of keys, a public key and a private key: what one encrypts, the other decrypts. The sender obtains the receiver’s public key, encrypts the plaintext with it, and sends the ciphertext; the receiver decrypts it with their private key. Because of the one-way nature of the function, other senders who also hold the public key cannot read each other’s messages.
41.	What is Asymmetric Encryption?
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
Asymmetric (public key) cryptography uses a pair of related keys, one public and one private, to encrypt and decrypt a message. The sender takes the recipient’s public key from a public directory to encrypt, and only the recipient’s private key (kept secret) can decrypt it. If the sender encrypts with their private key, only their public key can decrypt it, which authenticates the sender. These processes happen automatically.
42.	What is characteristics of stream cipher?
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
A stream cipher encrypts data one byte (bit) at a time by applying a key and algorithm to each binary digit of the plaintext stream. It is symmetric: the same key encrypts and decrypts. Key points:
•	It follows a pseudorandom number stream.
•	A longer key gives stronger security.
•	It needs fewer lines of code than a block cipher.
43.	How to choose block size in block chipher?
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
•	Avoid a very small block size  – with m bits there are 2ᵐ possible plaintext blocks, and an attacker can build a dictionary of plaintext/ciphertext pairs; a larger block makes this harder.
•	Avoid a very large block size  – the cipher becomes inefficient and plaintext needs padding.
•	Use a multiple of 8 bits  – easier to implement, as processors handle data in multiples of 8 bits.
44.	What is characteristics of block cipher?
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
A block cipher encrypts data in fixed-size blocks (commonly 64 or 128 bits) using a key and algorithm, processing one whole block at a time; the output block has the same length as the input block. It is an invertible symmetric-key algorithm (the same key encrypts and decrypts). Its strength depends on the key length, not on the block size.
45.	What is  advantages and challenges of Symmetric encryption?
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
•	Advantages  – scalability (low computational overhead), simplicity, speed and efficiency.
•	Challenges  – key distribution is difficult; if the key is lost or compromised all messages are vulnerable (hard key management); not ideal for large-scale communication with many parties; limited security if the key is weak or poorly managed.
46.	Differences between Monoalphabetic and Polyalphabetic Cipher
Mövzu: Kriptoqrafiya
•	Monoalphabetic cipher  – a substitution cipher in which each plaintext character is always replaced by the same ciphertext character throughout (e.g. a shift of three: ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ → DEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZABC).
•	Polyalphabetic cipher  – the replacement varies with the key and the character’s position, using several substitution alphabets (e.g. DEF… for cipher 1, GHI… for cipher 2, JKL… for cipher 3).
47.	What is signature-based detection in antivirus software?
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
The antivirus compares a file’s code with a dictionary of known virus signatures. On a match it can repair the file, quarantine it or delete it.
It cannot detect new malware or variants of existing malware until the definition file is updated, so it requires periodic (usually online) updates.
48.	What are the types of computer worms
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
1.	Email worms  – send outbound messages to all addresses in a user’s contact list.
2.	File-sharing worms  – copy themselves into shared folders and spread through peer-to-peer networks.
3.	Crypto worms  – encrypt data on the victim’s system.
4.	Internet worms  – spread to other devices the infected computer connects to via the internet and private networks.
5.	Instant messaging worms  – spread through attachments or links in chat messages sent to the contact list.
49.	What are the types of spyware?
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
•	Keylogger  – records everything typed (passwords, card data, websites visited); hard to detect.
•	Browser hijacker  – changes browser settings without the user’s consent.
•	Adware  – serves deceptive or annoying ads, with or without the user’s knowledge.
•	Web beacon  – tracking pixel or web bug that monitors activity (content viewed, emails opened).
•	Rootkit  – tools that give threat actors remote access and control.
•	Stalkerware  – used to track someone’s location.
50.	What are the types of trojan horses?
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
•	Backdoor Trojan  – gives an attacker remote access and control.
•	Ransom Trojan  – impairs the computer or blocks data until the user can no longer use it.
•	Fake antivirus Trojan  – simulates legitimate antivirus software.
•	Rootkit Trojan  – conceals itself so other malware stays active longer.
•	Game-thief Trojan  – steals account information of online gamers.
•	Banker Trojan  – targets banking accounts and financial information.
51.	What are the types of computer viruses?
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
•	Boot sector viruses  – affect the boot sector of a floppy, USB or hard disk.
•	Polymorphic viruses  – encrypt themselves differently (different algorithms and keys) on every infection.
•	Direct action viruses  – carry out specific actions, such as infecting and modifying files in a directory.
•	Overwrite viruses  – destroy data by overwriting it with random data.
•	Logic bombs  – not viruses but camouflaged program segments that destroy data once certain conditions are met.
•	Macro viruses  – infect files created by applications that contain macros.
52.	What is computer virus? Write signs that may help you identify computer-virus
Mövzu: Zərərli proqramlar və antiviruslar
A computer virus is a self-replicating program written to alter the way a computer operates without the user’s permission or knowledge. Signs of infection:
•	Slow system – applications take longer to open.
•	Too many pop-up windows.
•	Programs or files open by themselves in the background.
•	Being logged out of accounts, as accounts may be hacked.
•	The device crashes.
53.	What are email hijacking and session hijacking attacks?
Mövzu: Kiber hücumlar
•	Email hijacking  – attackers take control of the email accounts of a business or organization (often banks or credit card companies), monitor communications, and collect personal data and intelligence on transactions.
•	Session hijacking  – attackers steal session cookies to impersonate a user or obtain the information they hold (passwords, card numbers, account data); they must act fast because the cookie expires with the session.
54.	Which the difference between Distributed Denial-of-service attack and Denial-of-service attack?
Mövzu: Kiber hücumlar
1.	Detection/mitigation  – a DoS comes from one location, so its origin is easy to trace and block (a good firewall can do it); a DDoS comes from many remote locations that disguise its origin.
2.	Speed  – a DDoS can be deployed much faster, which makes it harder to detect.
3.	Traffic volume  – a DDoS uses many bots, sending far more traffic at once.
4.	Execution  – a DDoS coordinates infected hosts in a botnet controlled by a C&C server; a DoS typically uses a script or tool on one machine.
5.	Tracing  – tracing the source of a DDoS is much more complicated.
55.	What are types of phishing attack?
Mövzu: Kiber hücumlar
•	Bulk email phishing  – mass spam emails sent to as many people as possible, often posing as large legitimate businesses.
•	Spear phishing  – targets a specific individual with privileged access or authority (e.g. a finance manager).
•	Whale phishing (whaling)  – targets C-level and senior executives who can authorize large payments or release sensitive information.
•	Smishing  – SMS phishing with fake text messages, e.g. posing as a wireless provider offering a “free gift” or asking for card details.
56.	What is the Cyber-attack? Categorize the Cyber-attacks
Mövzu: Kiber hücumlar
Attacks generally fall into four categories:
•	Interception  – unauthorized access to data, applications or environments (file viewing, eavesdropping, reading e-mail); mainly an attack on confidentiality and hard to detect.
•	Interruption  – assets become unusable or unavailable, temporarily or permanently; mainly availability (e.g. DoS on a mail server), sometimes integrity.
•	Modification  – tampering with an asset; mainly integrity, possibly availability.
•	Fabrication  – generating data, processes or communications in a system (e.g. spurious database records); mainly integrity.
57.	What is consolidation of security infrastructure?
Mövzu: Kibertəhlükəsizlik
Consolidation means replacing many separate, independently managed security products (firewall, antivirus, VPN, web filter, email gateway, IPS from different vendors) with fewer integrated platforms, ideally managed from a single console, where functions share data, policies and management. Examples:
•	UTM / NGFW  – one appliance combining firewall, IPS, antivirus, VPN and content filtering.
•	SIEM  – collects logs from all systems in one place for analysis.
•	XDR  – correlates threat data across endpoints, network, email and cloud.
58.	How the MITRE ATT&CK Framework Helps Security-Teams?
Mövzu: Kibertəhlükəsizlik
MITRE ATT&CK is a systematic knowledge library of adversary tactics and techniques that helps security teams understand and defend against threats by providing:
•	Enhanced threat intelligence
•	Improved incident response capabilities
•	Effective prioritization of security controls
•	Streamlined communication among stakeholders
•	Benchmarking of cybersecurity maturity
•	Enhanced security awareness training
